# Coolify — Storage paths
DATA_ROOT=../../service_data/data/coolify

# Generate ALL of these once and never change them afterward — changing any
# of them after first install can break the installation (documented upstream
# behavior, not a guess):
#   openssl rand -hex 16                                          -> APP_ID
#   echo "base64:$(openssl rand -base64 32)"                       -> APP_KEY
#   openssl rand -base64 32   (x3, one each)                       -> DB_PASSWORD, REDIS_PASSWORD
#   openssl rand -hex 32      (x3, one each)                       -> PUSHER_APP_ID, PUSHER_APP_KEY, PUSHER_APP_SECRET
APP_ID=your_random_app_id
APP_KEY=base64:your_random_app_key
DB_PASSWORD=your_strong_password
REDIS_PASSWORD=your_strong_password
PUSHER_APP_ID=your_random_id
PUSHER_APP_KEY=your_random_key
PUSHER_APP_SECRET=your_random_secret

# Root user seeding — only takes effect on a fresh database (skipped if a
# user with id 0 already exists). Both ROOT_USER_EMAIL and
# ROOT_USER_PASSWORD must be set for this to run at all; password needs 8+
# chars, mixed case, a number, and a symbol (validated against
# HaveIBeenPwned — Coolify refuses a compromised password outright).
# Login is by email, not a fixed username. Setting these on an
# already-running instance does nothing.
# ROOT_USERNAME=Root User
# ROOT_USER_EMAIL=admin@example.net
# ROOT_USER_PASSWORD=change_me_before_first_start

# No other env vars to document here — Coolify manages everything else
# (including public registration on/off) through its own admin UI
# (Settings -> Configuration) rather than env vars. IMPORTANT operational
# note: public self-registration is ON by default and stays on until you
# manually disable it there after your first login — leaving it on with the
# UI reachable means anyone who finds the URL/port can create an account.
